abstract = {Human reliability analyses represent an integral part of the total reliability analyses of a human–machine system. The accidents in Bhopal, Three Mile Island, Chernobyl, and Piper Alpha are examples of human failures, which indicate that such failures can have catastrophically severe consequences. According to Moore (1994), approximately 65% of all catastrophic marine-related accidents are attributable to compounded human and organizational errors during operation. Consequently, risk assessment must be performed considering the risks associated with human error, and methods to alleviate the system vulnerability to such errors must be identified. To this end, human reliability assessment (HRA) can be performed, which is widely applied in many fields, such as design, fabrication, installation, and operation.}
